The magnifying power of a compound microscope is

\(m = m_o \times m_e \approx \dfrac{L}{f_o}\left(1 + \dfrac{D}{f_e}\right)\)

Reading off which quantities appear:

- the **focal lengths** \(f_o\) and \(f_e\) both appear — so magnification depends on them;
- the **tube length** \(L\) appears — so it matters too;
- the **colour of the light** enters indirectly, since the refractive index of the glass varies with wavelength and so therefore do both focal lengths — this is chromatic aberration, and it is why different colours are magnified slightly differently;
- the **aperture** of either lens appears nowhere.

So the magnification does **not** depend on the aperture of the objective and the eyepiece — option (A).

The aperture is far from irrelevant to the instrument, which is what makes it a good distractor. A larger aperture gathers more light, giving a **brighter** image, and it improves the **resolving power**, letting finer detail be separated. But brightness and resolution are different properties from magnification. An image can be magnified enormously and still be too dim to see, or magnified without revealing any new detail — "empty magnification", which is exactly what a large magnification with a small aperture produces.
